+
+## Creating a JSON Patch
+
+```ts
+import { createPatch } from "json-toolkit";
+
+const before = { servers: [{ host: "a.example" }, { host: "b.example" }], debug: true };
+const after = { servers: [{ host: "a.example" }, { host: "c.example" }], debug: true, log: "warn" };
+
+createPatch(before, after);
+// [
+//   { op: "replace", path: "/servers/1/host", value: "c.example" },
+//   { op: "add", path: "/log", value: "warn" },
+// ]
+```
+
+- `createPatch(from, to)` returns a JSON Patch (RFC 6902) that `applyPatch` turns `from` into `to`
+  with, so that a client can send what changed instead of the whole new version. Two equal documents
+  give an empty patch. Neither input is modified, and the patch shares no value with them.
+- The patch describes only what differs: unchanged members and elements are left out, and a change is
+  made where it is, deep inside the document. Array elements are aligned, so inserting or removing one
+  element does not rewrite the elements after it.
+- Where replacing a value whole is shorter, once serialized, than patching its parts, the patch uses
+  one `replace` instead. A value whose type changes (an object that becomes an array, a number that
+  becomes a string) is always replaced.
+- The patch uses `add`, `remove` and `replace` only, and its operations must be applied in order.
+- It throws an `InvalidDocumentError` when `from` or `to` is not a JSON value, as for `applyMergePatch`.
+
+See [ADR 0004](docs/adr/0004-json-patch-creation.md) for the choices made.
